<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwangmu Reform</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Gwangmu reforms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gwangmu Reform was the modernisation programme of the Korean Empire, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Emperor Gojong</a>'s era name Gwangmu, used from 1897 to 1907. Seeking to strengthen the state and the throne, the government surveyed land and issued land titles, founded schools, banks and factories, and introduced electricity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, a tramway and the first railway. The reforms were cut short when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1905.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Gabo government that had proclaimed the end of the status system collapsed, a loyalist cabinet was formed in 1896, and it became the Gwangmu government once the Korean Empire was proclaimed. It took systematic steps against the old class order. A new household register recorded each member's occupation instead of hierarchical status, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheonmin">cheonmin</a>, low-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> commoners who were mostly serfs and slaves, had to take a surname to register their own households; some adopted their masters' surnames, others the commonest local one. Reformers spoke of the people as inmin and later as kukmin, citizens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1897 the government began a cadastral survey, hiring American surveyors so that title deeds called jigye could record the exact size of each plot. It was tied to a reform of the land tax led by Yi Yong-ik, who also reformed the currency, and was halted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904 and 1905, with about two-thirds of the land surveyed. The army grew from about 5,000 soldiers to 28,000, its core a 1,000-strong royal guard trained by Russian officers from 1896 and armed with Berdan rifles. Western dress became the official uniform of civil officials in 1900, and later of soldiers and police.</p><p>In 1898 the emperor authorised joint ventures with American businessmen, among them the Hanseong Electric Company, which ran public lighting and an electric streetcar, and the first long-distance public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> followed in 1902. The state supported technical and industrial schools and weaving factories, opened elementary and secondary schools, and set up a Sanitary Board for vaccination, sanitation and quarantine, with licences for herbal doctors and pharmacists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Higher education began in these years: Severance Hospital Medical School in 1904, Bosung College under Yi Yong-ik in 1905, and Soongsil, recognised as a university in 1907. Under the Education Ordinance of 1911 issued by the Japanese Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> these institutions lost their college status. The reforms are seen as having prepared the ground for later Korean development in infrastructure and the economy and as the nucleus of a modern bureaucracy and army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kwangmu Reform” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Gwangmu reforms</b> carried out land surveys, issued land titles and brought electricity, telegraphs, a tramway and the first railway to Seoul, while strengthening imperial power.</blockquote><a href="/state/korean-empire">Korean Empire</a></li></ul></section>
